Here's how to handle all three scenarios:

### 1. Detect the current system theme

```csharp
AppTheme current = Application.Current!.RequestedTheme;
// Returns AppTheme.Light, AppTheme.Dark, or AppTheme.Unspecified
```

### 2. Subscribe to real-time theme changes

```csharp
Application.Current!.RequestedThemeChanged += (s, e) =>
{
    AppTheme newTheme = e.RequestedTheme;
    Console.WriteLine($"Theme changed to: {newTheme}");
    // Update UI or swap ResourceDictionaries here
};
```

### 3. Force a specific theme (ignore system setting)

```csharp
// Force dark mode
Application.Current!.UserAppTheme = AppTheme.Dark;

// Revert to following the system
Application.Current!.UserAppTheme = AppTheme.Unspecified;
```

### ⚠️ Critical Android requirement

Your `MainActivity` **must** include `ConfigChanges.UiMode` or the `RequestedThemeChanged` event won't fire — Android will restart the activity instead:

```csharp
[Activity(Theme = "@style/Maui.SplashTheme",
          MainLauncher = true,
          ConfigurationChanges = ConfigChanges.ScreenSize
                               | ConfigChanges.Orientation
                               | ConfigChanges.UiMode  // ← Required
                               | ConfigChanges.ScreenLayout
                               | ConfigChanges.SmallestScreenSize
                               | ConfigChanges.Density)]
public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity { }
```

### Persisting user preference

```csharp
// Save
Preferences.Set("AppTheme", "Dark");

// Restore on startup (e.g., in App constructor)
Application.Current!.UserAppTheme = Preferences.Get("AppTheme", "System") switch
{
    "Light" => AppTheme.Light,
    "Dark"  => AppTheme.Dark,
    _       => AppTheme.Unspecified
};
```

For theme-aware XAML, use `AppThemeBinding` for automatic light/dark switching:

```xml
<Label Text="Hello"
       TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light=Green, Dark=Red}" />
```

Or `DynamicResource` with swappable `ResourceDictionary` files for full custom themes.
